cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use Lunar Site Planner or its data, please cite it as below."
title: "Lunar Site Planner: terrain-aware Sun, Earth, power and flight planning for lunar landing sites"
type: software
authors:
  # Add teammates the same way; affiliation and orcid ("https://orcid.org/…") are optional.
  - given-names: "Ammar Rasyid"
    family-names: "Bin Remy Azrai"
  - name: "Team Dell Monitors (NASA Space Apps Challenge 2026, Kuala Lumpur)"
version: "1.0.0"
date-released: "2026-11-19"
license: MIT
repository-code: "https://github.com/Arairo/IMPROVED_lunarmoon-"
url: "https://arairo.github.io/IMPROVED_lunarmoon-/"
keywords:
  - Moon
  - lunar south pole
  - landing site selection
  - illumination
  - LOLA
  - Artemis
abstract: >-
  A browser tool that tells you, for a lander at a given site and window, when
  the Sun and Earth are above the real terrain skyline, how much power and
  radio contact that gives, and how to fly there. Skylines come from NASA LRO
  LOLA terrain (20 m PGDA tiles near the main south-polar sites), the Sun and
  Earth from astronomy-engine with the IAU lunar rotation model. Checked
  against JPL Horizons, NASA Goddard PGDA lighting maps, five Apollo landings
  and flown CLPS and international missions (see docs/VALIDATION.md).
